  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Wednesday...\n\nWeak shortwave w/ high pwats (0.8\" west to 1.2\" east) continues \nto produce showers and some embedded weak t-storms early this \nmorning. Large scale subsidence arrives from the west between \n18-00z so expect afternoon convection to be of less coverage, \nespecially given the cooling influence from the morning showers. \nSPC still has our east highlighted for a marginal risk (level \n1/5) of severe t-storms, with wind being the main risk. Heavy rain\nand marginally severe hail also possible during the afternoon. \nBrunt of activity across the area should be finished by 00z or \nshortly thereafter (i.e. a much earlier end expected today).\n\nThe air mass will be drier tomorrow but a trailing weak shortwave\nseems to be enough for isolated weak t-storms Friday afternoon and\nearly evening, mainly in the southeast. In fact, we may see some \nweak convection late tonight into Friday morning (models trending\nin that direction). With sufficient moisture in place any weak \nforcing of late has been generating at least weak convection. Look\nfor high temps in the upper 80s to mid 90s today, then 90s to \nnear 100F Friday.\n\nSaturday will be very hot, dry and deeply mixed ahead of a\ndeepening trof along the BC coast. It looks as if monsoon moisture\nwill be scoured out so no t-storms expected. 700mb temp plume of\n+16C to +20C should push temps over 100F across the area. The \nextreme heat will be a health risk for folks outdoors, so please \nplan accordingly. Have issued an Extreme Heat Watch across our \nlower elevations from noon to midnight. The triple digit heat and \n10% or less humidity will elevate fire weather concerns. Mixed \nwinds will be somewhat breezy during the peak heating hours but \nmid level flow is not strong (10-20kts). Also, a weak cold frontal\npassage is expected late Saturday night. Overall, the timing of \nthe fropa and stronger winds aloft do not coincide with peak \nheating and this should keep fire weather concerns from being more\ntroublesome.\n\nSunday will be cooler but still hot/dry, with breezy north winds \nover the east. A stronger frontal passage arrives Sunday night \nsetting the stage for notably cooler days Monday & Tuesday. The \nentire period from Saturday through Sunday night will see elevated\nfire weather concerns w/ dry conditions and periods of gusty \nwinds, and no precipitation.\n\nA few light showers are possible along the upper trof axis Monday,\notherwise dry conditions will prevail through the middle of next \nweek. High temps Monday & Tuesday look to be in the mid 70s to mid\n80s. Flat ridging should push temps back to near 90F Wednesday.\n\nSmoke impacts: Post-frontal subsidence and shift to upslope winds\nmay yield higher concentrations of surface-based smoke Saturday \nnight into Sunday then Sunday night into Monday. Poorer air \nquality is possible. Something to monitor over the next few days.\n\nClimate tidbits: Smoke permitting, there is a very good chance \nthat daily records will be broken this Saturday August 1st. \nBillings, Livingston & Sheridan may get close to August records as\nwell. These are summarized below.\n\nSITE      8/1 FORECAST   8/1 RECORD      AUG RECORD\nBillings     105         102 (2008)    105 (8/2/2024)\nLivingston   104         101 (2000)    105 (8/5/1961)\nMiles City   105         104 (2008)    110 (8/7/1995)\nSheridan     106         103 (2008)    106 (8/7/1983)\nBaker        102          99 (2022)    108 (8/12/2018)\n\nJKL",
